#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055} :activity/id #uuid "4918d79b-bb18-5ebc-b1d6-c9cd4c5a9bf1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862 :topic/id #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" :topic/math-academy-id 3932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p$, we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$.\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4cf166b-07f1-5e2e-9b9a-8147d42ce348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9526 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ce0e2df-ecaa-5cb0-b322-b2b78e5030ce" :step/math-academy-id 187404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189501 :tutorial/content "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$:\n\n> *Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n> - $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n> - $np > 5$, *and*\n> - $n(1-p) > 5$.\n> *Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable $\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)$.*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5$,\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1addf4e-37b8-58a0-827e-70f370944f92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9527 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Sample Distribution Model"} :step/id #uuid "32038366-e144-50bf-8a9e-d6ae30f4a820" :step/math-academy-id 187405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188193 :question/id #uuid "ddfa9789-7019-5939-b675-bcef59e5ef4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188685}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "672cee66-8553-5cd8-8135-323a5632bbce" :step/math-academy-id 187406 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188194 :question/id #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" :question/math-academy-id "e-15152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188684}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "4c971f78-cc05-5002-90c0-b319d1ef39e2" :step/math-academy-id 187409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188195 :question/id #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" :question/math-academy-id "e-15153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188681}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"} :step/id #uuid "69641840-a7d8-502b-b5fc-162ab4534b75" :step/math-academy-id 187412}] :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1